    <div class="roadmap-header fade-up fade-up-1">
        <h1>Roadmap</h1>
        <p class="dates">Updated 2026-04-21 &middot; Refreshed every couple of weeks</p>
    </div>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Here now</h2>
        <p>The self-hostable base shipped at launch. Every piece below installs with <code>parachute install &lt;service&gt;</code>.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Parachute Vault</strong> &mdash; the knowledge graph. SQLite-backed, REST + <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io" target="_blank">MCP</a>, wikilinks, full-text and semantic search, Obsidian import/export. <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-vault" target="_blank">Source &rarr;</a></li>
            <li><strong>Parachute Lens</strong> &mdash; browser-based companion for your vault. Installable PWA, works offline, voice capture with auto-transcription. <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-lens" target="_blank">Source &rarr;</a></li>
            <li><strong>Parachute Scribe</strong> &mdash; Whisper-compatible transcription API. Runs locally by default; BYO cloud provider if you want one. <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-scribe" target="_blank">Source &rarr;</a></li>
            <li><strong>Parachute Channel</strong> &mdash; messaging gateway that lets Claude Code agents reach you through Telegram, Discord, and other channels. <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-channel" target="_blank">Source &rarr;</a></li>
            <li><strong>Parachute CLI</strong> &mdash; the coordinator. <code>install</code>, <code>start</code>, <code>stop</code>, <code>expose</code>, lifecycle for every service. <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-cli" target="_blank">Source &rarr;</a></li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Next few weeks</h2>
        <p>Post-launch stabilization. Small, steady improvements driven by what early users run into.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Lens polish</strong> &mdash; quick-switcher, pinned and archived views, the today view, path tree. Mobile PWA refinement.</li>
            <li><strong>Vault atomic tag operations</strong> &mdash; rename and merge tags cleanly across a whole vault in one request, so clients don't have to orchestrate it.</li>
            <li><strong>Unified audio retention</strong> &mdash; control whether voice-memo originals stay on disk, stay until transcribed, or never persist, across every client at once.</li>
            <li><strong>Hub-hosted OAuth</strong> &mdash; one ecosystem sign-in that issues tokens carrying a service catalog, so clients discover what's installed rather than hard-coding paths. <a href="/docs/">Architecture sketch &rarr;</a></li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>A little further out</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Parachute Cloud</strong> &mdash; the done-for-you hosted path: managed vault, multi-device sync, cloud transcription, no server to run. Same shape as self-hosted, same portability. <a href="/docs/">Deployment sketch &rarr;</a></li>
            <li><strong>Native mobile wrapper</strong> &mdash; a Capacitor- or Tauri-wrapped build of Lens that unlocks background recording, on-device offline transcription, and pendant integration on phones. The PWA handles most of what most people need; the native wrapper handles the things a browser can't.</li>
            <li><strong>Configuration portal</strong> &mdash; the hub page renders per-module settings forms automatically from each module's JSON Schema. You won't need the CLI for most config changes.</li>
            <li><strong>Fine-grained scopes</strong> &mdash; clients ask for exactly what they need (read-only, read-write, per-vault, eventually per-folder). Foundation for third-party modules that users can trust partially.</li>
            <li><strong>Pendant</strong> &mdash; always-on voice capture hardware, writing directly to your vault. Prototype PCB and enclosure exist; a shippable v1 comes through the native-wrapper path.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Longer arc</h2>
        <p>Parachute is open-source and modular on purpose. The longer arc is less a timeline and more a shape: an ecosystem of small, composable modules that all speak a common contract, so you can mix the official ones with community ones with your own. The canonical architecture lives in <a href="/docs/">the design notes</a>.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Third-party modules</strong> &mdash; anyone can build a service that plugs into a Parachute hub. Calendar, email, chat, custom agents. The hub renders them like they were native.</li>
            <li><strong>AI reflections</strong> &mdash; longer-horizon synthesis over your vault (weekly digests, patterns across time), as a module you choose to install rather than a built-in.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

